root = TaxonomyBrowser(
  "Why Learners Can't Touch the Real System",
  [leastPrivilegeAccess, productionData, irreversibleActions, licenceEnvironmentCost],
)

leastPrivilegeAccess = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Least-Privilege Access",
  "No role, no practice",
  "ShieldCheck",
  "mauve",
  "Least-privilege access control means trainees often have no account or role that allows the actions they must learn.",
  "Good security gives each person only the permissions their current job needs. A new hire in the finance team may not get the 'approve payment' role until after training, and an IT trainee should never hold domain-admin rights just to practise. So the very actions the manual must teach are the ones the learner is not allowed to perform. A simulation lets them rehearse the approver's screens without ever holding the approver's role.",
  "A flight simulator: trainee pilots practise engine failures long before anyone hands them the keys to a real aircraft.",
  "Teaching actions the learner is not yet authorised to perform",
  [
    "Roles granted only after training or certification",
    "Admin and approver screens that trainees must not reach",
    "Separation-of-duties rules (one person cannot both request and approve)",
  ],
  ["Protecting the data inside the system (see Production Data)"],
)

productionData = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Production Data",
  "Real people's records are not a practice ground",
  "Database",
  "peach",
  "Real systems hold production data (customer PII, patient or financial records) that trainees must not see or change.",
  "A hospital system shows real patients, a CRM shows real customers, and a banking back office shows real balances. Letting trainees browse that data for practice breaks privacy law (such as GDPR or Thailand's PDPA) and the organisation's own policies. Even a read-only training account still exposes personal data to people who have no business need to see it.",
  "Medical students practise on mannequins, not on the patient in bed 12.",
  "Keeping personal and confidential records away from practice",
  [
    "Personally identifiable information (PII)",
    "Patient, financial and HR records",
    "Privacy and data-protection obligations",
  ],
  ["Actions that change the world outside the system (see Irreversible Actions)"],
)

irreversibleActions = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Irreversible Actions",
  "Some buttons cannot be un-pressed",
  "TriangleAlert",
  "red",
  "Some actions are irreversible or high-impact in a live system, such as issuing a refund, deleting a record or changing a network rule.",
  "A refund sends real money, a deleted record may be gone for good, an e-mail blast reaches real customers, and a wrong firewall rule can cut off a whole office. Learning by trial and error is how people learn software best, but in a live system every error is a real incident. A simulation removes the consequence while keeping the practice.",
  "Fire drills use a pretend fire so people can practise the exit route without anyone getting burned.",
  "Letting learners make mistakes that cost nothing",
  [
    "Money movement: refunds, payments, payroll",
    "Destructive actions: delete, purge, overwrite",
    "Outbound effects: e-mails, orders, network and security changes",
  ],
  ["Whether the learner is allowed to act at all (see Least-Privilege Access)"],
)

licenceEnvironmentCost = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Licence & Environment Cost",
  "A real seat for every trainee adds up",
  "Wallet",
  "yellow",
  "Per-seat licences and shared environments make it costly to give every trainee a real system to practise on.",
  "Enterprise software such as ERP, CRM or ITSM platforms is usually licensed per user, and extra non-production environments cost more again. A class of 200 new staff each needing a seat just to practise for a week is hard to justify. Shared test environments are also busy with developers and testers, who do not want trainees changing their data.",
  "A driving school doesn't buy every student a car; it shares a few dual-control cars and uses a simulator for the rest.",
  "Scaling practice without scaling licences",
  [
    "Per-user licensing of enterprise platforms",
    "Cost and upkeep of extra non-production environments",
    "Shared test environments that trainees would disrupt",
  ],
  ["The cost of building and maintaining the simulation itself (see Keeping It Alive)"],
)
